<section>
    <title>Operations with Functions</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x)=\dfrac{x}{x-2}</m> and
                    <m>g(x)=\dfrac{2x}{x-1}</m>.
                    Find
                    
            <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>(f-g)(-2)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>(f+g)(1)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>(f-g)(2)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\dfrac{f}{g}(3)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\dfrac{f}{g}(0)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>g\cdot g(3)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f\circ g(1)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f\circ g(2)=f(g(2))=f\left(\dfrac{2\cdot 2}{2-1}\right)=f(4)=\dfrac{4}{4-2}=2</m>.
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>g \circ f(x)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f \circ f (x)</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </p>
</statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>(f-g)(-2)=f(-2)-g(-2)=\dfrac{-2}{-2-2}-\dfrac{2(-2)}{-2-1}=\dfrac{1}{2}-\dfrac{4}{3}=-\dfrac{5}{6}</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>g(x)</m> is not defined for
                                        <m>x=1</m>, so <m>(f+g)(1)</m> is
                                        undefined.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f(x)</m> is not defined for
                                        <m>x=2</m>, so <m>(f-g)(2)</m> is
                                        undefined.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\dfrac{f}{g}(3)=\dfrac{f(3)}{g(3)}=\dfrac{\dfrac{3}{3-2}}{\dfrac{2\cdot 3}{3-1}}=\dfrac{3}{3}=1</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>g(0)=0</m>, so <m>\dfrac{f}{g}(0)</m>
                                        is undefined.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>g\cdot g(3)=g(3)\cdot g(3)=3\cdot 3=9.</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f\circ g(1)=f(g(1))</m>, but <m>g</m>
                                        is not defined at <m>1</m>, so
                                        <m>f\circ g(1)</m> is undefined.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>g\circ f(x)=g(f(x))=g\left(\dfrac{x}{x-2}\right)=\dfrac{2\cdot\dfrac{x}{x-2}}{\dfrac{x}{x-2}-1}=\dfrac{\dfrac{2x}{x-2}}{\dfrac{2}{x-2}}\\=\dfrac{2x}{x-2}\cdot \dfrac{2}{x-2}=x</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f\circ f(x)=f(f(x))=f\left(\dfrac{x}{x-2}\right)=\dfrac{\dfrac{x}{x-2}}{\dfrac{x}{x-2}-2}=\dfrac{\dfrac{x}{x-2}}{\dfrac{-x+4}{x-2}}=\dfrac{x}{4-x}</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Use the table to find the indicated values or state that
                    they cannot be determined based solely on the table.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Using the graphs of the function <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>
                    below, find the following values or state they cannot be
                    determined based on the image.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Write the functions as a composition of at least three
                    different functions.
                    Note that there are several ways to do this, so the answer
                    is not unique.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^{2}+1}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=3(x-1)^{3/2}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{\sqrt{(1+2x)^{3}}+2\sqrt{1+2x}-1-2x}{\sqrt{1+2x}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                            </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f=f_{1}\circ f_{2} \circ f_{3}</m>,
                                        where
                                        <m>f_{1}(x)=\dfrac{1}{x},f_{2}(x)=\sqrt{x},f_{3}(x)=x^{2}+1</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f=f_{1}\circ f_{2} \circ f_{3}</m>,
                                        where
                                        <m>f_{1}(x)=3x,f_{2}(x)=x^{3/2},f_{3}(x)=x-1</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f=f_{1}\circ f_{2} \circ f_{3}</m>,
                                        where
                                        <m>f_{1}(x)=1+2x,f_{2}(x)=\sqrt{x},f_{3}(x)=\dfrac{x^3+2x-x^2}{x}</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find the domain of the functions <m>f\circ g</m>,
                    <m>g\circ f</m>, <m>f\circ f</m>, <m>g\circ g</m>, if
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=, g(x)=</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Solving Inequalities</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find all the real values of <m>x</m> that satisfy the
                    following inequalities.
                    Write your answer using interval notation or set builder
                    notation.
                    
            <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>5x-3&gt;2x+1</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Elementary inequality rules
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>(x-2)^{2}\leq (x+1)^{2}</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
					Section: 1.8. 
					
					Subsection: Elementary inequality rules
				\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>x^{2}-4x+3\leq 0</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>x(x-1)&gt;(x-\dfrac{1}{2})^{2}</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>3x^{2}+5x+7&gt;0</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>3x^{2}+5x+7\leq 0</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}\geq 0</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}\geq 2</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>(x+1)(2x-1)^{2}(2-x)&lt;0</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\dfrac{(x+1)(2x-1)^2}{2-x}\leq 0</m>
                            </p>
                            <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
				Section: 1.8. 
				
				Subsection: Sign diagram technique
			\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                            
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\dfrac{x^2+8x+7}{x-1}&gt;2</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </p>
</statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        5x-3&amp;&gt;3x+1\\ 5x-2x&amp;&gt;1+3\\ 3x&amp;&gt;4\\ x&amp;&gt;\dfrac{4}{3}
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        The solution is
                                        <m>\left(\dfrac{4}{3},\infty\right)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        (x-2)^{2}&amp;\leq (x+1)^{2}\\ x^{2}-4x+3&amp;\leq x^{2}+2x+1\\ -4x-2x&amp;\leq 1-4\\ -6x&amp;\leq -3\\ x&amp;\geq \dfrac{1}{2}
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        The solution is
                                        <m>\left[\dfrac{1}{2},\infty\right]</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        We need to solve the inequality
                                        <m>x^{2}-4x+3\leq 0</m>.
                                        In other words, we need to find all
                                        <m>x</m> such that <m>g(x) \geq 0</m>,
                                        where <m>g(x)=x^{2}-4x+3</m>.
                                        The first step is to find the domain of
                                        <m>g(x)</m>.
                                        The domain of <m>g(x)</m> is
                                        <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
                                        Next, we find the zeroes by solving the
                                        equation <m>g(x)=0</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        x^{2}-4x+3&amp;=0\\ (x-1)(x-3)&amp;=0\\ x=1 \text{ or }x&amp;=3.
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        Both <m>1</m> and <m>3</m> are in the
                                        domain of <m>g(x)</m>, so they are both
                                        zeroes.
                                        Using this information, we can draw a
                                        sign chart.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <md>
                                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]\draw[-&gt;] (-5,0) -- (5,0);

\draw (-2.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{}; \draw (1.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{};

\node at (-2.5, .5) {1}; \node at (1.5, .5) {3};\node at (-5,.5) {$g\colon$}; \node at (-3.5,.5) {$+$}; \node at (-0.4,.5) {$-$}; \node at (3, .5) {$+$};\end{tikzpicture}
                                        </md>
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        Since we are trying to find all <m>x</m>
                                        such that <m>x^{2}-4x+3</m> is negative
                                        or equal to <m>0</m>, using the sign
                                        chart, the solution is <m>[1.3]</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        x(x-1)&amp;&gt;(x-1\dfrac{1}{2})^{2}\\ x^{2}-x&amp;&gt;x^{2}-x+\dfrac{1}{4}\\ 0&amp;&gt;\dfrac{1}{4}.
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        Since <m>0&gt;\dfrac{1}{4}</m> is false
                                        for any <m>x</m>, there is no real
                                        solution to this inequality.
                                        We say that the solution is
                                        <m>\emptyset</m> (the <em>empty set</em>
                                        ).
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        We need to solve the inequality
                                        <m>3x^{2}+5x+7&gt; 0</m>.
                                        In other words, we need to find all
                                        <m>x</m> such that <m>g(x) \geq 0</m>,
                                        where <m>g(x)=3x^{2}+5x+7</m>.
                                        However, this equation has no real
                                        solutions, so <m>g(x)</m> is either
                                        positive everywhere or negative
                                        everywhere.
                                        We notice that for all <m>x</m>,
                                        <m>g(x)&gt;0</m>.
                                        Hence, all real numbers satisfy this
                                        inequality, so the solution is
                                        <m>(-\infty,\infty)=\mathbb{R}</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        From the previous exercise, we notice
                                        there are no <m>x</m> such that
                                        <m>3x^{2}+5x+7\leq 0</m>, so the
                                        solution is <m>\emptyset</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        We need to solve the inequality
                                        <m>\dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}\geq 0</m>.
                                        In other words, we need to find all
                                        <m>x</m> such that <m>g(x) \geq 0</m>,
                                        where <m>g(x)=\dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}</m>.
                                        The first step is to find the domain of
                                        <m>g(x)</m>, which is all real numbers
                                        such that <m>x+2\neq0</m>.
                                        Thus, the domain of <m>g(x)</m> is
                                        <m>(-\infty, -2) \cup (-2, \infty)</m>.
                                        Next, we find the zeroes by solving the
                                        equation <m>g(x)=0</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        \dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}&amp;=0\\ 2x-1&amp;=0\\ x&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}.
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        <m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m> is in the domain of
                                        <m>g(x)</m>, so it is a zero.
                                        Using this information, we can draw a
                                        sign chart.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <md>
                                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]\draw[-&gt;] (-5,0) -- (5,0);

\draw (1.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{}; \draw[blue, thick, fill=white] (-2.5,0) circle (2.5pt);

\node at (-2.5, .5) {-2}; \node at (1.5, .5) {1/2};\node at (-5,.5) {$g\colon$}; \node at (-3.5,.5) {$+$}; \node at (-0.4,.5) {$-$}; \node at (3, .5) {$+$};\end{tikzpicture}
                                        </md>
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        Since we are trying to find all <m>x</m>
                                        such that <m>\dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}</m> is
                                        positive or equal to <m>0</m>, using the
                                        sign chart, we conclude that the
                                        solution is
                                        <m>(\infty, -2) \cup [1/2, \infty]</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}&amp;\geq 2\\ \dfrac{2x-1}{x+2}- 2&amp;\geq 0\\ \dfrac{2x-1-2x-4}{x+2}&amp;\geq 0\\ \dfrac{-5}{x+2}&amp;\geq 0
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        We need to solve the inequality
                                        <m>\dfrac{-5}{x+2}\geq 0</m>.
                                        In other words, we need to find all
                                        <m>x</m> such that <m>g(x) \geq 0</m>,
                                        where <m>g(x)=\dfrac{-5}{x+2}</m>.
                                        The first step is to find the domain of
                                        <m>g(x)</m>.
                                        The domain of <m>g(x)</m> is
                                        <m>(-\infty, -2) \cup (-2, \infty)</m>.
                                        Next, we find the zeroes by solving the
                                        equation <m>g(x)=0</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        \dfrac{-5}{x+2}&amp;=0\\ -5&amp;=0
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        so this equation has no solutions, and
                                        hence <m>g(x)</m> has no zeroes.
                                        This does NOT mean we cannot solve the
                                        inequality.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        We can still draw a sign chart.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <md>
                                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]\draw[-&gt;] (-4,0) -- (0,0);

\draw[blue, thick, fill=white] (-2,0) circle (2.5pt);

\node at (-2, .5) {-2};\node at (-5,.5) {$g\colon$}; \node at (-3.5,.5) {$+$}; \node at (-0.5,.5) {$-$};\end{tikzpicture}
                                        </md>
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        Since we are trying to find all <m>x</m>
                                        such that <m>\dfrac{-5}{x+2}</m> is
                                        positive or equal to <m>0</m>, using the
                                        sign chart, we conclude that the
                                        solution is <m>(-\infty,-2)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        We need to solve the inequality
                                        <m>(x+1)(2x-1)^{2}(2-x)&lt; 0</m>.
                                        In other words, we need to find all
                                        <m>x</m> such that <m>g(x) &lt; 0</m>,
                                        where <m>g(x)=(x+1)(2x-1)^{2}(2-x)</m>.
                                        The first step is to find the domain of
                                        <m>g(x)</m>.
                                        The domain of <m>g(x)</m> is
                                        <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
                                        Next, we find the zeroes by solving the
                                        equation <m>(x+1)(2x-1)^{2}(2-x)=0</m>.
                                        The solutions are
                                        <m>x=-1,x=\dfrac{1}{2},x=2</m> and all
                                        three solutions are in the domain.
                                        We draw a sign chart.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <md>
                                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]\draw[-&gt;] (-7,0) -- (3,0);

\draw (-2,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{}; \draw (-4.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{}; \draw (0.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{};

\node at (-2, .5) {1/2}; \node at (-4.5, .5) {$-1$}; \node at (0.5, .5) {2}; \node at (-7,.5) {$g\colon$}; \node at (-3.25,.5) {$+$}; \node at (-0.75,.5) {$+$}; \node at (1.75, .5) {$-$}; \node at (-5.75, .5) {$-$};\end{tikzpicture}
                                        </md>
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        Since we are trying to find all <m>x</m>
                                        such that <m>(x+1)(2x-1)^{2}(2-x)</m> is
                                        negative, using the sign chart, we
                                        conclude that the solution is
                                        <m>(-\infty,-1)\cup (2,\infty)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        We need to solve the inequality
                                        <m>\dfrac{(x+1)(2x-1)^2}{2-x}\leq 0</m>.
                                        In other words, we need to find all
                                        <m>x</m> such that <m>g(x) \leq 0</m>,
                                        where
                                        <m>g(x)=\dfrac{(x+1)(2x-1)^2}{2-x}</m>.
                                        The first step is to find the domain of
                                        <m>g(x)</m>.
                                        The domain of <m>g(x)</m> is
                                        <m>\mathbb{R}\setminus \{2\}</m>.
                                        Next, we find the zeroes by solving the
                                        equation
                                        <m>\dfrac{(x+1)(2x-1)^2}{2-x}=0</m>.
                                        The solutions are
                                        <m>x=-1,x=\dfrac{1}{2}</m> and both are
                                        in the domain.
                                        We draw a sign chart.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <md>
                                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]\draw[-&gt;] (-7,0) -- (3,0);

\draw (-2,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{}; \draw (-4.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{};\draw[blue, thick, fill=white] (0.5,0) circle (2.5pt);

\node at (-2, .5) {1/2}; \node at (-4.5, .5) {$-1$}; \node at (0.5, .5) {2}; \node at (-7,.5) {$g\colon$}; \node at (-3.25,.5) {$+$}; \node at (-0.75,.5) {$+$}; \node at (1.75, .5) {$-$}; \node at (-5.75, .5) {$-$};\end{tikzpicture}
                                        </md>
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        Since we are trying to find all <m>x</m>
                                        such that
                                        <m>\dfrac{(x+1)(2x-1)^2}{2-x}\leq 0</m>
                                        is negative or 0, using the sign chart,
                                        we conclude that the solution is
                                        <m>(-\infty,-1]\cup (2,\infty)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \dfrac{x^2+8x+7}{x-1}&gt;2&amp;\iff \dfrac{x^2+8x+7}{x-1}-2&gt;0\\&amp;\iff \dfrac{x^2+8x+7}{x-1}-\dfrac{2x-2}{x-1}&gt;0\\&amp;\iff \dfrac{x^2+6x+9}{x-1}&gt;0\\&amp;\iff \dfrac{(x+3)^2}{x-1}&gt;0
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        We can use a sign chart as previously to
                                        solve the inequality.
                                        However, notice that
                                        <m>(x+3)^{2}\geq 0</m> for all
                                        <m>x\in\mathbb{R}</m>, so we only need
                                        to find the <m>x</m> such that
                                        <m>x-1&gt;0</m> i.e., <m>x&gt;1</m>.
                                        Thus, the solution is <m>(1,\infty)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Determine whether the following statements are true or
                    false.
                    Justify your reasoning.
                </p>
                <TODO type="unknown-environment"> <!--todo: unknown environment "reading"--> <pre>\begin{reading}
			Section: 1.8. 
		\end{reading}</pre> </TODO>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m> are such that
                                <m>a\leq b</m>, then <m>a^{2}\leq ab</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m> are such that
                                <m>a\leq b</m>, then <m>a^{3}\leq 2a^{2}</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m> are such that
                                <m>a&lt; b</m>, then <m>a^{3}&lt; a^{2}b</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}</m> are
                                such that <m>a&lt; b</m>, then
                                <m>\dfrac{1}{a}&gt;\dfrac{1}{b}</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m> are such that
                                <m>0&lt;a&lt; b</m>, then
                                <m>\dfrac{1}{a}&gt;\dfrac{1}{b}</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                            </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        False.
                                        Multiplying both sides of an inequality
                                        by a negative number changes the sign.
                                        For example, take <m>a=-1,b=1</m>.
                                        Then <m>a=-1\leq 1=b</m>, but
                                        <m>a^{2}=1&gt;-1=ab</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        True.
                                        <m>a^{2}</m> is either positive or
                                        <m>0</m>.
                                        If <m>a^{2}&gt;0</m>, multiplying both
                                        sides of the inequality by <m>a^{2}</m>
                                        will not change the sign.
                                        If <m>a^{2}=0</m>, i.e.
                                        <m>a=0</m>, then we get
                                        <m>a^{3}=0\leq0=2a^{2}</m> which is also
                                        true.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        False.
                                        If <m>a=0, b=1</m>, it is not true that
                                        <m>a^{3}=0 &lt; 0=a^{2}b</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        False.
                                        If <m>a=-1, b=1</m>, it is not true that
                                        <m>\dfrac{1}{a}=-1 &gt; 1=\dfrac{1}{b}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        True.
                                        If we multiply both sides of the
                                        inequality by <m>ab&gt;0</m>, then we
                                        get a true statement <m>b&gt;a</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Domains of Algebraic Functions</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find the domain of the following algebraic functions.
                </p>

                <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\sqrt{|3-2x|-1}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{(x-1)(x-4)}{\sqrt{x^{2}-4x+3}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{(x-1)^{3/2}-8}{x^2-6x+5}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        To find the domain, we need to solve the
                                        inequality <m>|3-2x|-1\geq 0</m>, i.e.
                                        <m>|3-2x|&gt;1</m>.
                                        Hence, <m>3-2x&gt;1</m> or
                                        <m>3-2x&lt;-1</m>.
                                        Solving these inequalities, we get
                                        <m>x&lt;1</m> or <m>x&gt;2</m>.
                                        Thus, the domain of the function is
                                        <m>(-\infty,1]\cup[2,\infty)</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        To find the zeroes, we need to solve the
                                        equation <m>\sqrt{|3-2x|-1}</m>, i.e.
                                        <m>|3-2x|-1=0</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        |3-2x|-1=-&amp;\iff |3-2x|=1\\&amp;\iff 3-2x=1 \text{ or }3-2x=-1\\&amp;\iff x=1 \text{ or }x=2
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        Both 1 and 2 are in the domain, so both
                                        are zeroes of the function.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        To find the domain, we need to solve the
                                        inequality <m>x^{2}-4x+3&gt;0</m>.
                                        Using a sign chart,
                                        <md>
                                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]\draw[-&gt;] (-5,0) -- (5,0);

\draw (-2.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{}; \draw (1.5,0) node[circle,blue,fill,inner sep=2pt]{};

\node at (-2.5, .5) {1}; \node at (1.5, .5) {3};\node at (-3.5,.5) {$+$}; \node at (-0.4,.5) {$-$}; \node at (3, .5) {$+$};\end{tikzpicture}
                                        </md>
                                        we conclude that the domain is
                                        <m>(-\infty,1)\cup(3,\infty)</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        To find the zeroes, we need to solve the
                                        equation
                                        <m>f(x)=\dfrac{(x-1)(x-4)}{\sqrt{x^{2}-4x+3}}</m>,
                                        i.e.,
                                        <m>(x-1)(x-4)=0\iff x=1 \text{ or }x=4</m>.
                                        But 1 is not in the domain of the
                                        function, so the only zero is 4.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The domain of the function is all
                                        <m>x</m> such that
                                        <m>x-1\geq 0\iff x\geq 1</m> and such
                                        that <m>x^{2}-6x+5\neq 0</m>, i.e.
                                        <m>(1,5)\cup(5,\infty)</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        To find the zeroes, we need to solve the
                                        equation
                                        <m>\dfrac{(x-1)^{3/2}-8}{x^2-6x+5}=0</m>,
                                        i.e., <m>(x-1)^{3/2}-8=0</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        (x-1)^{3/2}-8=0&amp;\iff (x-1)^{3/2}=8\\&amp;\iff x-1=8^{2/3}\\&amp;\iff x-1=4\\&amp;\iff x=5
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        But 5 is not in the domain of the
                                        function, so the function has no zeroes.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Piecewise Defined Functions and Absolute Value</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Sketch the graph of the given function and evaluate it at
                    the given points.
                    
            <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=</m>
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>g(x)=</m>
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </p>
</statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find all the real values of <m>x</m> that satisfy the
                    following inequalities.
                    Write your answer using interval notation or set builder
                    notation.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\left| 2x+1\right|\leq 3</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\left|3x-2\right|&gt;4</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\left| 2x+1\right|\leq 0</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\left| 2x+1\right|&lt; 0</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\left| 2x+1\right|\geq 0</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\left| 2x+1\right|&gt;0</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                            </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Note that you can use a sign chart to
                                        solve these exercises.
                                        <md>
                                            \left| 2x+1\right|\leq 3
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            -3&lt;2x+1&lt;3
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            -4&lt;2x&lt;2
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            -2&lt;x&lt;1
                                        </md>
                                        Thus, the solution is the interval
                                        <m>(-2,1)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <md>
                                            \left|3x-2\right|&gt;4
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            3x-2&gt;4 \text{ or }3x-2&lt;-4
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            3x&gt;6 \text{ or }3x&lt;-2
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            x&gt;2 \text{ or }x&lt;-\dfrac{2}{3}
                                        </md>
                                        Thus, the solution is
                                        <m>(-\infty,-\dfrac{2}{3})\cup(2,\infty)</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Note that there are no <m>x</m> such
                                        that <m>|2x+1|&lt;0</m>, so we only need
                                        to solve the equation <m>|2x+1|=0</m>.
                                        <md>
                                            |2x+1|=0
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            2x+1=0
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            x=-\dfrac{1}{2}
                                        </md>
                                        Thus, the solution is the set
                                        <m>\{-\dfrac{1}{2}\}</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Since there are no such <m>x</m> such
                                        that <m>|2x+1|&lt;0</m>, the solution is
                                        <m>\emptyset</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Since <m>|2x+1|\geq0</m> for all
                                        <m>x</m>, the solution is
                                        <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>|2x+1|&gt;0</m> for all <m>x</m>
                                        except when <m>|2x+1|=0</m>, i.e.,
                                        <m>x\in\mathbb{R}\setminus \{-1/2\}=(-\infty,-1/2)\cup(-1/2,\infty)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Determine whether the following statements are true or
                    false.
                    Justify your reasoning.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>|a+b|=|a|+|b|</m> for all real numbers
                                <m>a,b</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>|ab|=|a||b|</m> for all real numbers
                                <m>a,b</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\left|\dfrac{a}{b}\right|=\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}</m>
                                for all real numbers <m>a,b</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>x</m> is a real number such that
                                <m>|x-2|&lt;3</m>, then <m>|3x-6|&lt;10</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>x</m> is a real number such that
                                <m>|3x-6|&lt;10</m>, then <m>|x-2|&lt;3</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                            </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        False.
                                        For example, the equality is not true if
                                        <m>a=-1, b=1</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        True.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        False.
                                        If <m>b=0</m>, neither side of the
                                        equality is defined.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        True.
                                        Multiply both sides of <m>|x-2|&lt;3</m>
                                        by <m>3</m> to get <m>3|x-2|&lt;9</m>,
                                        which is equivalent to
                                        <m>|3x-6|&lt;9</m>.
                                        But <m>9&lt;10</m>, so
                                        <m>|3x-6|&lt;10</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        False.
                                        If we try to use the same idea as before
                                        and multiply by <m>1/3</m>, we get
                                        <m>|x-2|&lt;\dfrac{10}{3}</m>, but
                                        <m>\dfrac{10}{3}&gt;3</m>.
                                        Hence, any value of <m>x</m>, such that
                                        <m>3&lt;|x-2|&lt;10/3</m> will make this
                                        statement false.
                                        For example, if you take <m>x=5.2</m>,
                                        <m>|3\cdot 5.2-6|&lt;10</m>, but
                                        <m>|5.2-2|&gt;3</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    The graphs of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> are given below.
                    Sketch the graphs of <m>|f(x)|</m> and <m>|g(x)|</m>.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Introduction to Limits</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find the limits based on the graph or state that they do not
                    exist.
                    Justify your answer.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Sketch the graph of the piecewise defined function.
                    Determine the limits based on the graph.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Limits by Definition</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Use the <m>\epsilon</m>- <m>\delta</m> definition of a limit
                    to show the following statements.
                    
            <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 3} (x-1)}=2</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 0} (2x+1)}=1</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} (-2x+1)}=-1</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 2} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}=4</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </p>
</statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Using the notation
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to a} f(x)}=L</m>,
                                        <m>f(x)=x-1, a=3, L=2</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            |f(x)-2|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |(x-1)-2|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |x-3|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        Thus, we take <m>\delta=\epsilon</m>.
                                        Hence, for all <m>\epsilon&gt;0</m>, let
                                        <m>\delta=\epsilon</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            0&lt;|x-1|&lt;\epsilon \Rightarrow |(x-1)-2|&lt;\epsilon,
                                        </md>
                                        which proves that
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 3} (x-1)}=2</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Using the notation
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to a} f(x)}=L</m>,
                                        <m>f(x)=2x+1, a=0, L=1</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            |f(x)-1|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |(2x+1)-1|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |2x|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |2||x|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            2|x|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |x|&lt;\epsilon/2
                                        </md>
                                        Thus, we take <m>\delta=\epsilon/2</m>.
                                        Hence, for all <m>\epsilon&gt;0</m>, let
                                        <m>\delta=\epsilon/2</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            0&lt;|x|&lt;\epsilon/2 \Rightarrow |(2x+1)-1|&lt;\epsilon,
                                        </md>
                                        which proves that
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 0} (2x+1)}=1</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Using the notation
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to a} f(x)}=L</m>,
                                        <m>f(x)=-2x+1, a=1, L=-1</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            |f(x)-(-1)|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |(-2x+1)-(-1)|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |-2x+2|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |-2(x-1)|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |-2||x-1|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            2|x-1|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |x-1|&lt;\epsilon/2
                                        </md>
                                        Thus, we take <m>\delta=\epsilon/2</m>.
                                        Hence, for all <m>\epsilon&gt;0</m>, let
                                        <m>\delta=\epsilon/2</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            0&lt;|x-1|&lt;\epsilon/2 \Rightarrow |(-2x+1)-(-1)|&lt;\epsilon,
                                        </md>
                                        which proves that
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} (-2x+1)}=-1</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Using the notation
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to a} f(x)}=L</m>,
                                        <m>f(x)=\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}, a=2, L=4</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            |f(x)-2|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            \left|\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}-4\right|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            \left|\dfrac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}-4\right|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |x+2-4|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        <md>
                                            |x-2|&lt;\epsilon
                                        </md>
                                        Thus, we take <m>\delta=\epsilon</m>.
                                        Hence, for all <m>\epsilon&gt;0</m>, let
                                        <m>\delta=\epsilon</m>.
                                        We have
                                        <md>
                                            0&lt;|x-2|&lt;\epsilon \Rightarrow \left|\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}-4\right|&lt;\epsilon,
                                        </md>
                                        which proves that
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 2} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}=4</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Let <m>\epsilon=1</m>.
                    Find a <m>\delta&gt;0</m> that satisfies the
                    <m>\epsilon-\delta</m> definition of the limit
                    <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2} (4x-1)}=7</m>.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            Using the notation
                            <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to a} f(x)}=L</m>, we have
                            <m>f(x)=4x-1,a=2,L=7</m>.
                            <md>
                                |f(x)-L|&lt;\epsilon
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                |4x-1-7|&lt;1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                |4x-8|&lt;1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                |4(x-2)|&lt;1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                4|x-2|&lt;1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                |x-2|&lt;0.25
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Thus, any <m>\delta &lt;0.25</m> satisfies the
                            <m>\epsilon-\delta</m> definition of the given
                            limit.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Let <m>\epsilon=1</m>.
                    Find a <m>\delta&gt;0</m> that satisfies the
                    <m>\epsilon-\delta</m> definition of the limit
                    <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-1} (x^2-2x)}=3</m>.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            Using the notation
                            <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to a} f(x)}=L</m>, we have
                            <m>f(x)=x^{2}-2x,a=-1,L=3</m>.
                            <md>
                                |f(x)-L|&lt;\epsilon
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                |x^{2}-2x-3|&lt;1
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Limit rules</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Compute the following limits.
                    Write the solution as a chain of equalities with steps
                    justified by the limit rules and/or evaluation rules.
                    You do not have to justify the purely algebraic steps where
                    you do not use any of the limit/evaluation rules.
                    
            <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 1} \left(\dfrac{2x}{x-1}-\dfrac{-5x+1}{x^2-4x+3}\right)}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 9} \dfrac{x^2-9x}{3-\sqrt{x}}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x-1}{x-x^{-1}}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 2} \dfrac{x^2-3x+2}{(x^2-4)(1-\sqrt{x})}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x^2-3x+2}{(x^2-4)(1-\sqrt{x})}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 0} \dfrac{x^2-3x+2}{(x^2-4)(1-\sqrt{x})}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to1} \dfrac{x^2+xa-x-a}{x^2+x-2}}</m>,
                                where <m>a\in\mathbb{R}</m>.
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 2} \dfrac{(x-2)(x^2-x-2)}{(x-2)^2}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </p>
</statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <TODO type="unknown-macro"> <!--todo: unknown macro "\item"--> <c>\item</c> </TODO>
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 1} \left(\dfrac{2x}{x-1}-\dfrac{-5x+1}{x^2-4x+3}\right)}&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 1} \dfrac{2x}{x-1}-\dfrac{-5x+1}{(x-1)(x-3)}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 1} \dfrac{2x(x-3)-(-5x+1)}{(x-1)(x-3)}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 1} \left(\dfrac{2x^2-x-1}{(x-1)(x-3)}\right)}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 1} \dfrac{(x-1)(2x+1)}{(x-1)(x-3)}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 1} \dfrac{2x+1}{(x-3)}}\\&amp;=-\dfrac{3}{2}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 9} \dfrac{x^2-9x}{3-\sqrt{x}}}&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 9} \dfrac{x^2-9x}{3-\sqrt{x}}}\ \dfrac{3+\sqrt{x}}{3+\sqrt{x}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 9} \dfrac{x(x-9)(3+\sqrt{x})}{9-x}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 9} \dfrac{x(x-9)(3+\sqrt{x})}{-(x-9)}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 9} \dfrac{x(3+\sqrt{x})}{-1}}&amp;\text{ (replacement rule)}\\&amp;= \dfrac{9(3+\sqrt{9})}{-1}&amp;\text{ (alg. function eval.)}\\&amp;= -54
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x-1}{x-x^{-1}}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x-1}{x-\dfrac{1}{x}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x-1}{\dfrac{x^2-1}{x}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x(x-1)}{x^2-1}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x}{x+1}}&amp;\text{(replacement rule)}\\&amp;= \dfrac{1}{1+1}&amp;\text{(alg. function eval.)}\\&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 2} \dfrac{x^2-3x+2}{(x^2-4)(1-\sqrt{x})}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 2} \dfrac{(x-1)(x-2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(1-\sqrt{x})}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 2} \dfrac{x-1}{(x+2)(1-\sqrt{x})}}&amp;\text{(replacement rule)}\\&amp;= \dfrac{2-1}{(2+2)(1-\sqrt{2})}&amp;\text{(alg. function eval.)}\\&amp;= \dfrac{1}{4(1-\sqrt{2})}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x^2-3x+2}{(x^2-4)(1-\sqrt{x})}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{x^2-3x+2}{(x^2-4)(1-\sqrt{x})}}\  \dfrac{1+\sqrt{x}}{1+\sqrt{x}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{(x^2-3x+2)(1+\sqrt{x})}{(x^2-4)(1-x)}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{(x-1)(x-2)(1+\sqrt{x})}{-(x^2-4)(x-1)}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 1} \dfrac{(x-2)(1+\sqrt{x})}{-(x^2-4)}}&amp;\text{(replacement rule)}\\&amp;= \dfrac{(1-2)(1+\sqrt{1})}{-(1^2-4)}&amp;\text{(alg. function eval.)}\\&amp;= -\dfrac{2}{3}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 0} \dfrac{x^2-3x+2}{(x^2-4)(1-\sqrt{x})}}&amp;= \dfrac{0^2-3\times 0 +2}{(0^2-4)(1-\sqrt{0})}&amp;\text{(alg. function eval.)}\\&amp;= -\dfrac{1}{2}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to1} \dfrac{x^2+xa-x-a}{x^2+x-2}}&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to1} \dfrac{x(x+a)-(x+a)}{(x+2)(x-1)}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to1} \dfrac{(x+a)(x-1)}{(x+2)(x-1)}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to1} \dfrac{x+a}{x+2}}\\&amp;=\dfrac{1+a}{3}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 2} \dfrac{(x-2)(x^2-x-2)}{(x-2)^2}}&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 2} \dfrac{x^2-x-2}{x-2}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 2} \dfrac{(x-2)(x+1)}{x-2}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 2} (x+1)}\\&amp;=3
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Continuity and the IVT</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Based on the graph, find all points where the function is
                    not continuous.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Show that the function <m>f(x)=x^{3}+3x-2</m> has a root
                    that is positive.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            Since <m>f(0)=-2&lt;0, f(1)=2&gt;0</m> and the
                            function is continuous on the interval <m>[0,1]</m>,
                            by the IVT, there is a <m>c\in (0,1)</m> such that
                            <m>f(c)=0</m>.
                            Thus, <m>c</m> is a positive root of the function.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Show that the function <m>f(x)=</m> has a positive and a
                    negative root.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Show that the equation <m>2x^{3}-2x=2x^{2}-1</m> has three
                    distinct solutions on the interval <m>[-2,2]</m>.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Show that the equation <m>3x^{2}=x^{1/3}+1</m> has at least
                    two distinct solutions on the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Infinite Limits and Limits at Infinity</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Compute the following limits.
                    
            <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x^2-2}{2x^2+3x+5}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to \infty} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to -\infty} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x-1}{\sqrt{4x^{2}+1}}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to \infty}\dfrac{3\sqrt{x}-2x}{5\sqrt{x}+7x}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x-1}{\sqrt{4x^{2}+1}}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} (x-\sqrt{x^{2}+2x})}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to \infty}(\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}-\sqrt{x^{2}+3})}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to \infty}(\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}+\sqrt{x^{2}+3})}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2^+}\dfrac{x}{x-2}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 5^-} \dfrac{x^2+1}{7x-10-x^2}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 2^-} \dfrac{x^2-5x+6}{x^2-4x+4}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-2^+} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x+2}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </p>
</statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x^2-2}{2x^2+3x+5}}&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x^2(1-\dfrac{2}{x^2})}{x^2(2+\dfrac{3}{2x}+\dfrac{5}{x^2})}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{1-\dfrac{2}{x^2}}{2+\dfrac{3}{2x}+\dfrac{5}{x^2}}}\\&amp;=\dfrac{\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} 1}-\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{2}{x^2} }}{\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty}2}+\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{3}{2x}}+\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{5}{x^2}}}\\&amp;=\dfrac{1-0}{2+0+0}\\&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x^2(1-\dfrac{4}{x^2})}{x(1-\dfrac{2}{x})}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} x}\\&amp;=\infty
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x^2(1-\dfrac{4}{x^2})}{x(1-\dfrac{2}{x})}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} x}\\&amp;=-\infty
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x-1}{\sqrt{4x^{2}+1}}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x(1-\dfrac{1}{x})}{\sqrt{x^{2}(4-\dfrac{1}{x^2})}}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x(1-\dfrac{1}{x})}{|x|\sqrt{4-\dfrac{1}{x^2}}}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x(1-\dfrac{1}{x})}{x\sqrt{4-\dfrac{1}{x^2}}}}\\&amp;= \dfrac{1}{4}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{3\sqrt{x}-2x}{5\sqrt{x}+7x}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{3x^{1/2}-2x}{5x^{1/2}+7x}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x(\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}}-2)}{x(\dfrac{5}{x^{1/2}}+7)}}\\&amp;=-\dfrac{2}{7}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x-1}{\sqrt{4x^{2}+1}}}&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x(1-\dfrac{1}{x})}{\sqrt{x^{2}(4-\dfrac{1}{x^2})}}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x(1-\dfrac{1}{x})}{|x|\sqrt{4-\dfrac{1}{x^2}}}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x(1-\dfrac{1}{x})}{-x\sqrt{4-\dfrac{1}{x^2}}}}\\&amp;= -\dfrac{1}{4}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} (x-\sqrt{x^{2}+2x})}&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} (x-\sqrt{x^{2}+2x})\cdot \dfrac{x+\sqrt{x^{2}+2x}}{x+\sqrt{x^{2}+2x}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{x^2-(x^2+2x)}{x+\sqrt{x^{2}+2x}}}\\&amp;= \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-2x}{x(1+\sqrt{1+\dfrac{2}{x}})}}\\&amp;=-2
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        \displaystyle{\lim_{x\to \infty}(\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}-\sqrt{x^{2}+3})}&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to \infty}(\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}-\sqrt{x^{2}+3})\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}+\sqrt{x^{2}+3}}{\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}+\sqrt{x^{2}+3}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{(x^2+4x)-(x^2+3)}{\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}+\sqrt{x^{2}+3}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{4x-3}{\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}+\sqrt{x^{2}+3}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x(4-\dfrac{3}{x})}{x(\sqrt{1+\dfrac{4}{x}}+\sqrt{1+\dfrac{3}{x^2}})}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{4}{1+1}}\\&amp;=2
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to \infty}(\sqrt{x^{2}+4x}+\sqrt{x^{2}+3})}=\infty</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2^+}\dfrac{x}{x-2}}=\infty</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 5^-} \dfrac{x^2+1}{7x-10-x^2}}=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 5^-} \dfrac{x^2+1}{(5-x)(x-2)}}=-\infty</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to 2^-} \dfrac{x^2-5x+6}{x^2-4x+4}}=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2^-}\dfrac{(x-2)(x-3)}{(x-2)^2}}=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2^-} \dfrac{x-3}{x-2}}=\infty</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-2^+} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x+2}}=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-2^+} \dfrac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x+2}}=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\-2^+} (x-2)}=-4</m>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find the horizontal and vertical asymptotes (if any) of the
                    following functions.
                    Use limits to justify your answers.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{2x^2}{x^2-1}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}-4}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                            </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{2x^2}{x^2-1}}=2</m>,
                                        so the function has a horizontal
                                        asymptote <m>y=2</m>.
                                        Since
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{2x^2}{x^2-1}}=2</m>,
                                        that is the only horizontal asymptote.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        We test for vertical asymptotes at the
                                        points where <m>x^{2}-1=0</m> i.e., at
                                        <m>x=\pm 1</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to1^-} \dfrac{2x^2}{x^2-1}}=-\infty</m>
                                        and this is enough to conclude that the
                                        function has a vertical asymptote
                                        <m>x=1</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to -1^-} \dfrac{2x^2}{x^2-1}}=\infty</m>
                                        and this is enough to conclude that the
                                        function has a vertical asymptote
                                        <m>x=-1</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}=\infty</m>
                                        and
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}=-\infty</m>,
                                        so the function has no horizontal
                                        asymptotes.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        We test for vertical asymptotes at the
                                        points where <m>x-2=0</m> i.e., at
                                        <m>x=2</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2^-} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}=\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2^+} \dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}}=4</m>,
                                        so the function has no vertical
                                        asymptotes.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to\infty} \dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}-4}}}=1</m>
                                        and
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-\infty} \dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}-4}}}=-1</m>,
                                        so the function has two horizontal
                                        asymptotes <m>y=-1</m> and <m>y=1</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        We test for vertical asymptotes at the
                                        points where <m>\sqrt{x^{2}-4}=0</m>,
                                        i.e., <m>x=\pm 2</m>.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        Note that the function is not defined
                                        for <m>x&lt;2</m> and <m>x&gt;-2</m>, so
                                        we cannot find the left hand limit at 2
                                        or the right hand limit at -2.
                                        Since
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to-2^-} \dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}-4}}}=-\infty</m>
                                        and
                                        <m>\displaystyle{\lim_{x\to2^+} \dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}-4}}}=\infty</m>,
                                        the function has 2 vertical asymptotes
                                        <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=2</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x)=\dfrac{x^4}{12}-\dfrac{x^2}{2}+2x</m>.
                    Find all <m>x</m> such that <m>f''(x)&gt;0</m>.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                f'(x)=\dfrac{4x^3}{12}-\dfrac{2x}{2}+2=\dfrac{x^3}{3}-x+2
                            </md>
                            and
                            <md>
                                f''(x)=\dfrac{3x^2}{3}-1=x^{2}-1.
                            </md>
                            Thus, we need to find all <m>x</m> such that
                            <m>x^{2}-1&gt;0</m>.
                            Using a sign chart, we conclude that the solution is
                            <m>(-\infty,-1)\cup(1,\infty)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Derivatives</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Compute the following using the limit definition of a
                    derivative.
                    
            <ol marker="(a)"><li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f'(x)</m> if <m>f(x)=x^{2}-x+1</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li>

            <li>

                            <p>
                                <m>f'(4)</m> if <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                                        </li></ol>
                            </p>
</statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0} \dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{[(x+h)^2-(x+h)+1]-[x^2-x+1]}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{x^2+2xh+h^2-x-h+1-x^2+x-1}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{2xh+h^2-h}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{h(2x+h-1)}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}(2x+h-1)}\\&amp;=2x-1
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(4)&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0} \dfrac{f(4+h)-f(4)}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0} \dfrac{\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{4+h}}-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{4}}}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{2-\sqrt{4+h}}{2h\sqrt{4+h}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{2-\sqrt{4+h}}{2h\sqrt{4+h}}\cdot \dfrac{2+\sqrt{4+h}}{2+\sqrt{4+h}}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{4-(4+h)}{2h\sqrt{4+h}(2+\sqrt{4+h})}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{-h}{2h\sqrt{4+h}(2+\sqrt{4+h})}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{-1}{2\sqrt{4+h}(2+\sqrt{4+h})}}\\&amp;=\dfrac{-1}{2\sqrt{4}(2+\sqrt{4})}\\&amp;=-\dfrac{1}{16}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Using the limit definition of the derivative, find the
                    equation of the tangent line to the function
                    <m>f(x)=(1+x)^{2}</m> at the point <m>(1,4)</m>.
                    Write your solution in the form <m>y=mx+b</m>.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <md>
                            m=f'(1)&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0} \dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{(1+1+h)^2-(1+1)^2}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{(2+h)^2-4}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{4+4h+h^2-4}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{4h+h^2}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{h(4+h)}{h}}\\&amp;=\displaystyle{\lim_{h\to 0}(4+h)}\\&amp;=4
                        </md>

                        <p>
                            Therefore, the equation of the tangent line is
                            <m>y-4=4(x-1)</m> or <m>y=4x</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Compute the first derivatives of the following functions.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=(2x+1)\sqrt{x}+\dfrac{1}{x}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{2x-1}{x^2-1}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{(x^{2}-1)^2}{x+2}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{(2x^{2}+3x-1)^{2025}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=(x-1)^{2}\sqrt{2x^{2}+1}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=[(2x+1)^{4}-\sqrt{x}]^{3}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\sqrt{x^{2}+1+\sqrt{x^2+1}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x)=\sqrt{\dfrac{x^2}{\sqrt{x^{2}+1}}}</m>
                                
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                            </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
<ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=(2x+1)'\sqrt{x}+(2x+1)\cdot \sqrt{x}'\\&amp;= 2\sqrt{x}+(2x+1)\dfrac{1}{2}x^{1/2-1}\\&amp;=2\sqrt{x}+\dfrac{2x+1}{2\sqrt{x}}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x))&amp;=\dfrac{(2x-1)'(x^2-1)-(2x-1)(x^2-1)'}{(x^2-1)^2}\\&amp;=\dfrac{2(x^2-1)-(2x-1)\cdot 2x}{(x^2-1)^2}\\&amp;=\dfrac{2x^2-2-4x^2+2x}{(x^2-1)^2}\\&amp;=\dfrac{-2x^2+2x-2}{(x^2-1)^2}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=\frac{(x+2)[(x^{2}-1)^{2}]'-(x^{2}-1)^{2}(x+2)'}{(x+2)^{2}}\\&amp;=\frac{(x+2)2(x^{2}-1)(x^{2}-1)'-(x^{2}-1)^{2}\cdot 1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\&amp;=\frac{4x(x^{2}-1)(x+2)-(x^{2}-1)^{2}}{(x+2)^{2}}\\&amp;=\frac{(x^{2}-1)\left[4x(x+2)-(x^{2}-1)\right]}{(x+2)^{2}}\\&amp;=\frac{(x^{2}-1)(3x^{2}+8x+1)}{(x+2)^{2}}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f(x)=(2x^{2}+3x-1)^{-2025}</m>, so
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=-2025(2x^{2}+3x-1)^{-2025-1}(2x^{2}+3x-1)'\\&amp;=-2025(2x^{2}+3x-1)^{-2026}(4x+3)\\&amp;=-\dfrac{2025(4x+3)}{(2x^2+3x-1)^{2026}}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f(x)=(x-1)^{2}(2x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>,
                                        so
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=[(x-1)^{2}]'(2x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}+(x-1)^{2}[(2x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}]'\\&amp;=2(x-1)^{2-1}(x-1)'(2x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}+(x-1)^{2}\cdot \dfrac{1}{2}(2x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}-1}(2x^{2}+1)'\\&amp;=2(x-1)\sqrt{2x^{2}+1}+\dfrac{2x(x-1)^2}{\sqrt{2x^{2}+1}}
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=3[(2x+1)^{4}-x^{\frac{1}{2}}]^{3-1}[(2x+1)^{4}-x^{\frac{1}{2}}]'\\&amp;=3[(2x+1)^{4}-x^{\frac{1}{2}}]^{2}[4(2x+1)^{4-1}(2x+1)'-\dfrac{1}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}-1}]\\&amp;=3[(2x+1)^{4}-\sqrt{x}]^{2}[8(2x+1)^{3}-\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}]
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f(x)=(x^{2}+1+(x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}})^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>,
                                        so
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}(x^{2}+1+(x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}})^{\frac{1}{2}-1}(x^{2}+1+(x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}})'\\&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}(x^{2}+1+(x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}})^{-\frac{1}{2}}(2x+\dfrac{1}{2}(x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}-1}(x^{2}+1)')\\&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}((x^{2}+1+(x^{2}+1)^{\frac{1}{2}})^{-\frac{1}{2}})(2x+x(x^{2}+1)^{-\frac{1}{2}})
                                    </md>
                                </li>
                        <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <m>f(x)=\left(\dfrac{x^2}{(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>,
                                        so
                                    </p>
                                    <md>
                                        f'(x)&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\dfrac{x^2}{(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}-1}\left(\dfrac{x^2}{(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}}\right)'\\&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\dfrac{x^2}{(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}}\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\dfrac{(x^2)'(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}-x^2[(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}]'}{x^2+1}\\&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\dfrac{x^2}{(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}}\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\dfrac{2x(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}-x^2\cdot \frac{1}{2}(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}-1}(x^2+1)'}{x^2+1}\\&amp;=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\dfrac{x^2}{(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}}\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}\dfrac{2x(x^2+1)^{\frac{1}{2}}-x^3(x^2+1)^{-\frac{1}{2}}}{x^2+1}
                                    </md>

                                    <p>
                                        You can also write <m>f(x)</m> as
                                        <m>f(x)=x(x^{2}+1)^{-\frac{1}{4}}</m>
                                        and go from there.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                        </ol>
</p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>
</section>

<section>
    <title>Implicit Differentiation</title>

    <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Assuming <m>y=y(x)</m> is a differentiable function, find
                    <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}</m> if <m>x^{2}y+xy^{2}=x-1</m>.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                x^{2}y(x)+x(y(x))^{2}=x-1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                \dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{2}y(x)+x(y(x))^{2}]=\dfrac{d}{dx}[x-1]
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                2xy(x)+x^{2}y'(x)+(y(x))^{2}+x\cdot 2y(x)y'(x)=1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                x^{2}y'+2xyy'=1-2xy-y^{2}
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                (x^{2}+2xy)y'=1-2xy-y^{2}
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                y'=\dfrac{1-2xy-y^2}{x^2+2xy}
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Assuming <m>x=x(y)</m> is a differentiable function, find
                    <m>\dfrac{dx}{dy}</m> if <m>x^{2}y+xy^{2}=x-1</m>.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                (x(y))^{2}y+x(y)y^{2}=x(y)-1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                \dfrac{d}{dy}[(x(y))^{2}y+x(y)y^{2}]=\dfrac{d}{dy}[x(y)-1]
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                2x(y)x'(y)y+x^{2}+x'(y)y^{2}+2xy=x'(y)
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                2xyx'+y^{2}x'-x'=-x^{2}-2xy
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                (2xy+y^{2}-1)x'=-x^{2}-2xy
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                x'=\dfrac{-x^2-2xy}{2xy+y^2-1}
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find the tangent line to the curve <m>x^{3}+y^{3}=6xy</m> at
                    the point <m>(3,3)</m>.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                x^{3}+(y(x))^{3}=6xy(x)
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                \dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{3}+(y(x))^{3}]=\dfrac{d}{dx}[6xy(x)]
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                3x^{2}+3y^{2}y'=6y+6xy'
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                3y^{2}y'-6xy'=6y-3x^{2}
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                (3y^{2}-6x)y'=6y-3x^{2}
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                y'=\dfrac{6x-3x^2}{3y^2-6x}.
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Thus, the slope of the tangent line at <m>(3,3)</m>
                            is
                            <md>
                                m=\dfrac{6\cdot 3-3\cdot 3^2}{3\cdot3^2-6\cdot 3}=-1.
                            </md>
                            Therefore, the equation of the tangent line is
                            <m>y-3=-1(x-3)</m> or <m>y=-x+6</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find the point(s) on the curve <m>x^{2}-xy+y^{2}=3</m> where
                    the tangent line is horizontal.
                    
                </p>
                            </statement>
<solution>
                        <p>
                            The tangent line is horizontal at points where
                            <m>y'(x)=0</m>.
                            <md>
                                x^{2}-xy(x)+(y(x))^{2}=3
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                \dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{2}-xy(x)+(y(x))^{2}]=\dfrac{d}{dx}[3]
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                2x-(y(x)+xy'(x))+2y(x)y'(x)=0
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                (-x+2y)y'=-2x+
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                y'=\dfrac{-2x+y}{-x+2y}
                            </md>
                            Thus, <m>y'=0</m> when <m>\dfrac{-2x+y}{-x+2y}=0</m>
                            i.e., when <m>-2x+y</m> or <m>y=2x</m>.
                            Substituting into the equation of the curve, we get
                            <md>
                                x^{2}-2x^{2}+4x^{2}=3
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                3x^{2}=3
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                x^{2}=1
                            </md>
                            <md>
                                x=1\text{ or }x=-1.
                            </md>
                            Therefore, the points on the curve where the tangent
                            is horizontal is <m>(1,2)</m> and <m>(-1,-2)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>

                <p>
                    Find the point(s) on the curve where the tangent line has
                    slope.
                </p>
                            </statement>
            </exercise>
</section>